## Interface: 20506
## Category: Commander
## Title: Commander Party Frames
## Author: Devin
## Version: 5.29.0
## Notes: Combat-first party frames built for arena: per-class layers that surface shields, hots, blessings, uptime, dispels, and CC status at a glance. Priests get the Power Word: Shield board with Renew and Prayer of Mending on a swept strip, and Mending is followed as the travelling object it actually is — one five-charge spell that lands, heals and jumps, with its charges read as five emptying lights, both ends of every hop lit, and an alert that fires only when the object ENDS (spent, expired, dispelled, or gone with a dead holder) and never on a bounce, carrying its charge count as five emptying lights AND the number, because how much is left and how much exactly are two different readings. Every noise the board can make is gathered into one Alert Sounds section per page — the shield break, the Lifebloom late warning, and Mending's two ends — each with the sound it plays, a picker, and a button to hear it, so two alerts can be told apart before the fight rather than during it; Mending defaults to a bell pair, one instrument at two pitches, rather than the stock raid warning half the client already uses; Mages get health/mana rows with total-absorb tracking, decurse and CC alarms, and a personal upkeep banner cut to four things — armor, Ice Block, Cold Snap, and what the team needs removed or freed; Druids get the hot board — Rejuvenation, Regrowth and a Lifebloom tracker built for rolling it (its own refresh window, a readable countdown instead of a radial sweep, stack pips, a drain bar under the health bar, and a late warning that fires only when you are actually behind), curse and poison alarms, and a form/cooldown banner; Paladins get the blessing board — one blessing slot per ally carrying the blessing you assigned them (per class, overridable per player, reassigned by clicking the slot itself), counted only when YOU cast it so a second paladin never makes a naked teammate look handled, with Greater satisfying the single; plus the Hands as a swept strip, the Forbearance lockout as a drain, and an aura/seal/cooldown banner whose aura and seal segments are click-to-switch. Every class gets a permanent ally-buff strip with per-buff tracking, and an opt-in urgency advisor that reddens a missing buff only when the target's situation and the enemy comp say it is actually costing you. Allies' pets get full ally rows too (on by default), so they can be buffed and healed like anyone else. On the click-cast board the row tooltip is its own setting: the game's unit tooltip with the board's notes under it, the notes alone (the default — a party frame is already telling you the name, health and buffs), or nothing at all; none of the three touch what makes @mouseover macros work. The sorted board has two orders besides urgency: Sort Rows can read the roster as a class list — warriors, paladins, hunters, rogues, priests, shamans, mages, warlocks, druids — with each class's members either closest to you first or alphabetical, and a pet under its owner's class, so the rows hold still while the fight changes and the nearest mage is always the first mage; Nearby Raid's own Row Order offers the same orders beside its nearest-first pick. Every ally carries a power strip for whatever they actually run on — mana, rage, energy, a pet's focus — on every board, and a FORECAST of what is already on its way to them: damage still queued in their dots, healing still rolling from their hots, and a heal in the air from anyone in the group. All three are learned from the combat log, so the numbers carry that caster's real gear, and all three are drawn however you like — as ghost fills on the health bar, as hairline projections, as numbers, or not at all. In a raid or a battleground there is Nearby Raid (off until you switch it on): the forty-person roster collapses back into party frames carrying you and the closest few allies — up to fifteen people — picked nearest-first. A control on the header re-picks on demand; right-clicking it leaves that on a timer, so the board re-sorts itself as people move around you — with just enough credit given to whoever is already on it that two allies either side of the cutoff do not swap in and out every few seconds. Its other half is the overheal ring: a hairline just inside any row where healing has spilled, brightening with how much of the heal was wasted. And every ally who is IN COMBAT wears the game's own crossed-swords mark — the glyph their portrait lights on every pull — at their row's right edge, dimmed when they are fighting somewhere you cannot reach: an ally already fighting is combat you can take off them with one heal — and a Sap does not open on a target in combat — while a board with no blades left on it is the one that says sit down, the drink will finish. It only ever marks the ones who ARE fighting, because an ally far enough out that the client is not tracking their unit reports no combat at all, and "out of combat" is not a claim this board can honestly make. In a battleground the same corner of the row picks up two more marks: whoever is CARRYING THE FLAG, and whichever ground powerup they are holding — Berserking, Restoration or Speed — with the flag always taking the first slot, because a carrier with Berserking up is still, first, the carrier. On a battleground board the question stops being who is hurt and becomes which of these ten people the next thirty seconds are about, and the carrier is a healing target with a different price on it than anyone else in the list — a price the powerup then changes again. The slots are held open only inside a battleground, since those auras cannot exist anywhere else and an arena row should keep that width for its health bar; and a carrier who runs out of your sight keeps the banner rather than losing it, which is the point, because running out of your sight is exactly what a flag carrier does. The banner across the top of every board is now yours to draw: a Header section on each page sets the strip's height and left inset, the size of the readout icons and the gap between them, the face, size and outline of every number it writes, the size of the controls at its right edge, and what sits behind it — five mixed-down tints or your own class colour at whatever opacity you like, with an optional hairline along its bottom edge for a board you want bounded without a slab of black over the world. Every default is the number the header hard-coded before the section existed, so a banner nobody configures is the banner it always was. And with Commander Production loaded, Production in Header puts its queue on that banner in place of the class cooldowns — every cooldown you are waiting on, soonest ready first, in the readout's own icon-and-countdown grammar — while the utility cluster, the gear, the rows toggle and the refresh stay exactly where they are; Production's own frame is not moved, this is a second view of the same queue.
